> I don't expect this case to fire normally

A typical example this can optimize would be this:

```c++
if (p_string != previous_string) {
    previous_string = p_string;
    recalculate_caches();
}
```

> this needs to be tested more in-situ to ensure it doesn't worsen performance when running normal data

There's no real cost to the change, since we had null checks anyway before (which run about as fast as inequality checks) :)
Int comparisons are so insanely fast that you may find it hard to even measure a difference when specifically testing for it.

> Yes but that's still a rare case, so that's why some deeper case analysis needs to be done to make sure it doesn't worsen performance etc., especially considering the thread safety stuff being carried out when fetching the pointers etc., the checks might also be extremely cheap but it also matters what the branching does, so this would be good to benchmark with `unlikely` for example to aid

We could certainly add `unlikely`! This would deoptimize the case where the pointers are both null, but this case is fast anyway, so it may be warranted to add a little cost to that to alleviate the others.

> I'd also say we might want to fetch the pointers once and then reuse them to avoid any issues

`ptr()` is free since it's guaranteed to be inlined :)

> But this IMO is a case where you can easily trick yourself with test data that isn't representative

Noted. I am very confident that this PR does not need extensive testing, but I'm happy to have others chip in.
To back my case, I have logged the amount of times the optimization is triggered during a normal launch:
```c++
bool String::operator==(const String &p_str) const {
	if (p_str.ptr() == ptr()) {
		if (p_str.ptr() != nullptr) {
			static int hits = 0;
			hits ++;
			print_line(hits);
		}
		// We have the same buffer (or are both null).
		return true;
	}
	if (length() != p_str.length()) {
		return false;
	}

	return memcmp(ptr(), p_str.ptr(), length() * sizeof(char32_t)) == 0;
}
```

This prints `53774` at the very end. Jesus christ, that surprises even me!

> And at minimum we'd need to know if it causes any benefits on real-world data, if it doesn't then that matters

I did run a benchmark to show that the change makes it faster :)
